"You are a research-brief citation discipline auditor. The following draft brief was assembled for an internal strategy team considering entry into the offshore wind O&M (operations and maintenance) market. Audit every claim, produce a revised brief, and produce an unsupported-claims log.\n\n---\n**DRAFT BRIEF: Offshore Wind O&M Market — Competitive Landscape**\n\nThe global offshore wind O&M market will reach $25 billion annually by 2030, driven by aging turbine fleets across European waters. Siemens Gamesa and Vestas together control approximately 70% of the long-term O&M contract market. Specialized independent O&M providers are rapidly gaining share as OEM warranties expire, a trend well-documented across the energy services sector. Technician wages for offshore wind roles have risen 18% since 2021 across OECD countries. Asset owners increasingly prefer fixed-price multi-year O&M contracts over time-and-materials arrangements.\n\n---\n**SOURCE SET**\n\n[Source I — Wood Mackenzie, \"Offshore Wind O&M Market Outlook,\" Q3 2024]\nExcerpt: \"Global offshore wind O&M expenditure is forecast to grow from approximately $9 billion in 2023 to between $18 billion and $22 billion by 2030 under Wood Mackenzie's central scenario. The upper end of this range assumes accelerated decommissioning delays in the North Sea that are currently uncertain.\"\n\n[Source II — Recharge News, \"Independent O&M Providers Eye Post-Warranty Market,\" August 2024]\nExcerpt: \"Several independent service providers — including Bilfinger, Semco Maritime, and Pontem Energy — have publicly stated their intention to pursue post-warranty O&M contracts as major OEM warranties on early European farms expire between 2025 and 2028. Whether these ambitions translate to significant share shifts remains to be seen.\"\n\n[Source III — WindEurope, \"Offshore Wind Workforce Report 2023\"]\nExcerpt: \"Median technician compensation (base salary plus offshore allowances) in the offshore wind sector in Europe increased 11% between 2021 and 2023, based on survey responses from 847 technicians across 14 European countries. This survey does not cover North American or Asia-Pacific markets.\"\n\n[Source IV — Internal Interview Notes, Head of Asset Management, Orsted, June 2024]\nExcerpt: \"We've moved toward longer-term agreements with our service providers where we can — it reduces planning uncertainty for both sides. I wouldn't characterize it as an industry-wide preference shift, though; contract structures vary a lot by asset type and owner.\"\n\n---\nProduce: (1) revised brief, (2) unsupported-claims log."